## A. Project overview

### A.1 Purpose

`axi2apb` is a synthesisable bridge that lets an AXI4-Lite master reach an APB3 peripheral. AXI4-Lite is the register-access flavour
of AXI (five channels, single beats, no bursts), and APB3 is the low-power peripheral bus (a two-phase transfer with wait states and an
error flag). The bridge converts one into the other: it accepts an AXI read or write, performs one APB transfer, and returns the AXI
response.

### A.2 Problem statement and motivation

The two protocols have different shapes. AXI has independent address, data and response channels, each with its own valid/ready
handshake, while APB has a single shared bus with a setup phase and an access phase that lasts until the slave raises PREADY. So a bridge
has to (1) serialise the AXI channels into APB phases, (2) hold the AXI response stable until the master takes it, (3) honour APB wait
states, and (4) turn the APB error signal into the right AXI response code. What I wanted from this project was to make that conversion
provably correct, because the original version of this bridge returned the wrong AXI response code for an APB error and nothing in its
testbench could notice.

### A.3 Scope and requirements

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| R1 | AXI4-Lite slave interface (AR, R, AW, W, B), `DATA_WIDTH` and `ADDR_WIDTH` parameters (default 32) |
| R2 | APB3 master interface (PSEL, PENABLE, PWRITE, PADDR, PWDATA, PREADY, PRDATA, PSLVERR) |
| R3 | Correct APB phases: a setup phase, then an access phase that lasts until PREADY; address, direction and write data stable throughout |
| R4 | AXI responses held until taken (BVALID/RVALID with stable BRESP/RDATA/RRESP until BREADY/RREADY) |
| R5 | APB error (PSLVERR) returned as SLVERR (2'b10); success as OKAY (2'b00); no other response code |
| R6 | One transaction at a time; a read request has priority over a simultaneous write request |
| R7 | Verified by a self-checking testbench, bound protocol assertions, coverage and an injected-bug campaign; synthesised and timed on Sky130 HD |

Out of scope: write strobes (the bridge has no WSTRB input, so every write is a full-word write), APB4 (PSTRB/PPROT), AXI PROT, more
than one outstanding transaction, and clock-domain crossing.

### A.4 Target application and concepts

The bridge connects low-speed peripherals (timers, UARTs, GPIO, register files) to an AXI interconnect. Building and checking it covers
the AMBA AXI4-Lite and APB3 handshake rules, protocol conversion with an FSM, wait-state handling, response mapping, protocol
assertions, coverage, mutation analysis, synthesis and timing.

### A.5 Key features and measurable objectives

- An 8-state Moore FSM. Without wait states the write path takes 5 cycles and the read path 4 cycles from IDLE back to IDLE (observed in
  simulation).
- 23 bound assertions and 17 named cover points, a 15-bug injection campaign, and the as-found RTL shown failing the new testbench
  (section G).
- Objective: every check passes on 8 seeds x 3000 random transactions plus the directed phases, every cover point and line is hit, and
  each synthesis mapping has a measured T_min and area.

---

## B. Architecture and block diagram

### B.1 Overview

The bridge is one module (`src/axi2apb.v`) with a control FSM, some output decode and a few registers. The FSM state decides which AXI
ready/valid signals and which APB signals are asserted, and the registers capture the address, drive PADDR/PWDATA, and hold the read data
and the AXI response. There's no FIFO and no skid buffer: the handshake with the AXI master is just the FSM waiting in the right state.

### B.2 Blocks

| Block | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Control FSM | `present_state` / `next_state`; arbitration (read first); wait for WVALID, PREADY, BREADY, RREADY |
| Output decode | ARREADY, AWREADY, WREADY, RVALID, BVALID, PSEL, PENABLE, PWRITE as functions of `present_state` (Moore outputs) |
| `sampled_addr` | latches ARADDR or AWADDR in IDLE |
| PADDR / PWDATA registers | drive the APB address and write data; loaded when the write data is accepted, or when a read starts |
| Response registers | RDATA <= PRDATA (if no error); BRESP / RRESP <= PSLVERR ? SLVERR : OKAY, at the PREADY cycle |
| `sampled_wdata` | a register that captures WDATA but whose output is not used (lint reports it; synthesis removes it) |

### B.3 Interfaces

| Group | Signals | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| clock/reset | `clk`, `rst_n` | synchronous active-low reset |
| AXI read | `S_AXI_ARADDR`, `ARVALID`, `ARREADY`, `RDATA`, `RRESP`, `RVALID`, `RREADY` | single beat |
| AXI write | `S_AXI_AWADDR`, `AWVALID`, `AWREADY`, `WDATA`, `WVALID`, `WREADY`, `BRESP`, `BVALID`, `BREADY` | AWREADY = WREADY (asserted together) |
| APB | `M_APB_PADDR`, `PSEL`, `PENABLE`, `PWRITE`, `PWDATA`, `PREADY`, `PRDATA`, `PSLVERR` | APB3 |

Parameters: `DATA_WIDTH` (32), `ADDR_WIDTH` (32).

### B.4 Data and control paths

**Data path**: `ARADDR`/`AWADDR` -> `sampled_addr` -> `PADDR`; `WDATA` -> `PWDATA`; `PRDATA` -> `RDATA`. **Control path**: the AXI valids,
`WVALID`, `PREADY`, `BREADY` and `RREADY` drive `next_state`, and the state drives the AXI readies and valids and the APB select, enable
and write signals. **Status path**: `PSLVERR` and `PREADY` come back from the slave and are registered into the response registers in the
PREADY cycle.

