## D. Design flowchart

![Figure D.1 - FSM and transfer flowchart: IDLE, write path (left), read path (right), wait loops and responses](diagrams/axi2apb_flowchart.png)

In IDLE, a read request (ARVALID) wins over a write request (AWVALID), and with neither the bridge stays in IDLE. A write goes through
WRITE_ADDRESS_RECEIVED (AWREADY = WREADY = 1, waiting for WVALID), WRITE_DATA_RECEIVED (the APB setup phase), PENABLE_WRITE (the APB
access phase, waiting for PREADY) and WDATA_TRANSFERRED (BVALID held until BREADY). A read goes through READ_ADDRESS_RECEIVED (ARREADY = 1
and the APB setup phase at the same time), PENABLE_READ (waiting for PREADY) and RDATA_TRANSFERRED (RVALID held until RREADY). Every "no"
branch means staying in the same state.

---

## E. Detailed design explanation

### E.1 Step-by-step operation

**Write.** (1) In IDLE the bridge sees AWVALID and latches the address. (2) In WRITE_ADDRESS_RECEIVED it asserts AWREADY and WREADY and
waits for WVALID; PADDR and PWDATA are loaded when WVALID arrives. (3) In WRITE_DATA_RECEIVED it asserts PSEL and PWRITE, which is the APB
setup phase. (4) In PENABLE_WRITE it adds PENABLE, and this access phase lasts until PREADY. (5) In the PREADY cycle BRESP is registered
from PSLVERR. (6) In WDATA_TRANSFERRED BVALID is asserted and held until BREADY, and then the FSM goes back to IDLE.

**Read.** (1) In IDLE the bridge sees ARVALID and loads PADDR with ARADDR. (2) In READ_ADDRESS_RECEIVED it asserts ARREADY and PSEL
together (the APB setup phase). (3) In PENABLE_READ it adds PENABLE until PREADY; RDATA is captured from PRDATA when there's no error, and
RRESP is registered from PSLVERR. (4) In RDATA_TRANSFERRED RVALID is asserted and held until RREADY.

These are the state sequences from the FSM definition, with no APB wait state, W valid together with AW, and a master that's ready at
once: a write goes IDLE -> WRITE_ADDRESS_RECEIVED -> WRITE_DATA_RECEIVED -> PENABLE_WRITE -> WDATA_TRANSFERRED -> IDLE (5 cycles), and a
read goes IDLE -> READ_ADDRESS_RECEIVED -> PENABLE_READ -> RDATA_TRANSFERRED -> IDLE (4 cycles). Each APB wait state adds one cycle in
PENABLE_WRITE or PENABLE_READ, and a slow master adds cycles in the response states.

### E.2 Response mapping and the fix made in this portfolio copy (CH-001)

The as-found RTL set BRESP and RRESP to `2'b11` when PSLVERR was high. In AXI, `2'b11` is DECERR, a decode error meaning there's no slave
at the address. An APB slave that signals an error is reporting a slave error, which is SLVERR, `2'b10`, and an APB bridge has no decode
error to report. I changed those two lines and nothing else in the RTL. Section G.7 shows the effect by running the as-found RTL against
the new testbench.

### E.3 Reset, latency, throughput and errors

- **Reset**: synchronous. The FSM goes back to IDLE and the address, data and response registers clear.
- **Latency**: 5 cycles for a write and 4 for a read from acceptance back to IDLE, plus any APB wait states and master back-pressure.
- **Throughput**: one transaction at a time, so at best one transfer every 4 or 5 cycles. The bridge is meant for register access, not
  bulk transfer.
- **Back-pressure**: BVALID and RVALID (with BRESP, RRESP and RDATA) are held until the master takes them, and the APB access phase is
  held until PREADY.
- **Errors**: an APB error becomes SLVERR on the matching response, and a read error doesn't update RDATA.

### E.4 Design decisions and trade-offs

| Decision | Alternative | Reason / cost |
|---|---|---|
| Moore FSM, one state per phase | Mealy outputs | glitch-free registered-state outputs, easy to assert on; costs a cycle per phase |
| Read priority in IDLE | round-robin | simple and documented; a continuous read stream could starve writes (not an issue for register access) |
| AWREADY = WREADY together | independent channels | a W that arrives before AW simply waits; avoids buffering write data |
| Single outstanding transaction | pipelined / outstanding | smallest bridge; sufficient for peripheral registers |
| Binary state encoding | one-hot | unchanged from the original; encoding was not part of the portfolio changes |

---

## F. Interface and timing details

### F.1 APB phases

A transfer is a **setup phase** (PSEL = 1, PENABLE = 0) of exactly one cycle, followed by an **access phase** (PSEL = PENABLE = 1) that
lasts until PREADY = 1. During the access phase PADDR, PWRITE and, for a write, PWDATA mustn't change. These rules, and the rule that
PENABLE drops after the transfer completes, are checked by assertions (section G.4).

### F.2 AXI response rules

Once BVALID is asserted it stays asserted, with BRESP stable, until BREADY; the same goes for RVALID with RDATA and RRESP until RREADY.
OKAY and SLVERR are the only legal responses.

### F.3 Clocking and reset

One clock domain, rising-edge flops and a synchronous active-low reset. There's no clock-domain crossing.

### F.4 Timing constraints used in synthesis

The constraints assume inputs arrive 0.35 ns after the clock edge and outputs are needed 0.15 ns before the next edge, with inputs driven
by a `buf_2` and an output load of 0.02 pF (`constraints/axi2apb.sdc`). Typical corner, ideal clock, no wires.

### F.5 Supported conditions and limitations

One outstanding transaction. Masters have to hold valid and the payload until ready, and the APB slave has to hold PRDATA and PSLVERR
valid in the cycle PREADY is high (APB3). A slave that asserts PREADY in the setup phase isn't covered.

---

## G. Verification and testing

### G.1 Objectives and test plan

Show that every read returns the last value written, that every response code is right, that the APB and AXI protocol rules are never
broken, that the stimulus reaches the interesting corners, and that the checks can fail.

### G.2 Testbench architecture

`tb_axi2apb` has five free-running channel processes (AW, W, B, AR, R), each fed from a queue with a delay per item. Around them sit an
APB slave model (`apb_slave_model`: 64 words, programmable wait states, and an error region where `paddr[11:8] == 4'hF`) and a scoreboard
with a 64-word reference memory and in-order expectation queues. The queue-fed processes replaced a first version built from `fork`/`join`
of tasks, which crashed Verilator 5.020.

### G.3 Test phases

(1) Directed writes and reads; (2) APB wait states of 1 to 3 cycles; (3) error responses for reads and writes, each followed straight away
by a good access; (4) back-pressure on B and R; (5) W before AW, and AW before W; (6) a read and a write requested together, then (6b)
pipelined bursts in which the next request arrives while the previous response is still pending; and (7) a constrained-random mix of
writes, reads, bursts and simultaneous read/write pairs, with random delays and wait states and an error region hit 15 % of the time.

### G.4 Assertions

`verif/axi2apb_checker.sv` holds 23 bound assertions. For APB3: `a_penable_needs_psel`, `a_setup_then_access`, `a_access_until_ready`,
`a_penable_drops`, `a_first_cycle_is_setup`, `a_addr_stable`, `a_dir_stable`, `a_wdata_stable` and `a_setup_to_access_same`. For the AXI
channels: `a_bvalid_holds`, `a_rvalid_holds`, `a_bresp_legal`, `a_rresp_legal`, `a_aw_w_ready_together` and `a_no_resp_both`. For the
response mapping: `a_bresp_maps_slverr`, `a_rresp_maps_slverr` and `a_rdata_is_prdata`. For sequencing: `a_one_outstanding`,
`a_bvalid_only_after_write` and `a_rvalid_only_after_read`. And for arbitration: `a_read_priority` and `a_write_when_no_read`. The 17 named
cover points cover writes and reads that end OK and in error, wait states, back-pressure, W-before-AW, AW-before-W, simultaneous AR/AW,
and back-to-back writes.

