## H. Synthesis, timing and performance results

{{include ../results/ppa.md}}

How to read the table: the timing-driven ABC script inserts fan-out buffers and sizes gates for delay, and Yosys' default
`abc -D 10000` does neither, so the same RTL maps to a much slower netlist. The comparison measures the tool flow, not a design change.
The CH-001 fix changes the area by a few square micrometres and doesn't change T_min. Power is **not reported**, because there's no
switching-activity file and OpenSTA's default activity isn't a measurement.

| Item | Status |
|---|---|
| Cells, area, flip-flops, T_min (pre-layout, typical corner) | measured (table) |
| Frequency in silicon, power, area after place and route | NOT MEASURED |
| An earlier "x % faster, y % lower power than the unoptimised netlist" statement | not reproducible from the original repository (no baseline netlist) and replaced by the measured comparison above |

---

## I. Results, limitations and future improvements

### I.1 Summary

With a two-line fix, the bridge passes a self-checking testbench with 23 bound assertions on 8 seeds, hits every cover point and every
line, catches 14 of 15 injected bugs (the 15th is equivalent), and has a measured synthesis and timing result. The as-found RTL fails the
same testbench on every error access.

### I.2 Most meaningful outcomes

1. A real specification bug found and fixed (DECERR instead of SLVERR), with evidence from before and after.
2. Protocol assertions that catch bugs the functional testbench can't see (APB setup-phase violations).
3. A measured synthesis comparison in place of unmeasured performance claims.

### I.3 Limitations

One outstanding transaction; no write strobes; APB3 only; pre-layout timing only; no power figure; and the AXI master and the APB slave
are the testbench's own.

### I.4 Future improvements

Support for WSTRB/PSTRB; outstanding transactions with a small response queue; a formal proof of the APB phase rules; one-hot state
encoding with a measured area and timing trade-off; and gate-level simulation with a switching-activity file for a real power figure.

### I.5 Interview talking points

- The APB setup and access phases, what PREADY does, and why PENABLE has to drop after the transfer.
- AXI response codes: why an APB error is SLVERR and not DECERR.
- Why AWREADY and WREADY are asserted together, and what that does to a master that sends W before AW.
- How an assertion catches a protocol bug that a data-checking testbench can't.
- Why the same RTL gives a 4.6 ns or a 1.7 ns period depending only on the ABC script, and what that says about reading synthesis
  numbers.

